# Alt 2 — Org to the top, user at the bottom

**Split the two concerns by position.** The org switcher becomes the sidebar's top workspace header (where you choose *where you are*); the bottom keeps a single user card (who you are + sign out). Both stay in the sidebar — just not stacked.

### Resting

```
┌───────────────────────────────┐
│ ▢ Acme Inc                ⌄  │  ← top: workspace header
├───────────────────────────────┤
│  Dashboard                    │
│  Concepts                     │
│  …nav…                        │
│                               │
├───────────────────────────────┤
│ (GD) Greg Daniels         ↗  │  ← bottom: user only
│      greg@acme.com            │
└───────────────────────────────┘
```

### Org header clicked (dropdown opens down)

```
┌───────────────────────────────┐
│ ▢ Acme Inc                ⌃  │
├───────────────────────────────┤
│  ✓ Acme Inc                   │
│    Globex Corp                │
│  + New organization           │
└───────────────────────────────┘
```

### Collapsed sidebar
Org icon pinned top, user avatar pinned bottom — natural top/bottom anchors.

```
┌────┐
│ ▢  │
├────┤
│ …  │
├────┤
│(GD)│
└────┘
```

**Pros:** bottom holds exactly one item; matches the mental model "workspace up top, me down bottom"; org switch stays one click; both visible at rest.
**Cons:** still two affordances overall (just relocated); top header takes vertical space that was a nav row.
